Experimental directed fixed-denominator count arithmetic.

This changes the interval enclosure and therefore is not an exact-output
optimization. It cannot reuse any old leaf acceptance. A new count forest
and fresh acceptance, or complete fresh acceptance of a compatible old
forest, is mandatory before any theorem claim.

An interval stores integers L,H representing [L/S,H/S], S=10^18. Every
rational constructor checks order before rounding its lower endpoint down
and upper endpoint up. Addition, subtraction and negation are exact on
stored scaled endpoints. For scalar multiplication the sign selects the
correct ordered endpoints and division by the scalar denominator is rounded
outwards. For interval multiplication, min/max of all four products are
rounded outwards after division by S. For a positive interval divisor all
four endpoint quotients are enclosed directly. The divisor's stored lower
endpoint must be strictly positive. Nonnegative integer powers use monotonic
endpoints for odd powers and the zero-crossing/min/max rule for even powers;
rescaling by S^(n-1) is again outward. Zero and unit powers are explicit.

Thus each operation encloses the exact range over its supplied intervals.
Substitution into an interval enclosure argument remains valid inductively.
A widened enclosure may fail a domain gate that the original passes; such a
failure is not an exclusion, and must stop or subdivide the calculation.
No gate is relaxed. Empty-intersection conclusions remain sound when taken
only from proved disjoint enclosures.

The existing optimized Bernstein transform first translates each interval
coefficient using exact rational weights. Its lower/upper endpoint choice
is determined by the weight sign. Each product is now rounded outwards to
the common denominator before summing. Degree-elevation weights are positive,
so outward multiplication of the resulting lower/upper bounds encloses the
same exact Bernstein coefficients. The returned Fraction values are the
outward coefficient bounds. This remains a valid enclosure even though it
can differ from the exact-rational transform.

The installer substitutes the interval class in every known imported module
alias, checking a single original class identity, and installs the outward
Bernstein vector. The ordinary rational count parameters, probability/mass
formulas, Taylor exponential helpers, directed roots, Gaussian enclosures,
count predicates and geometric resource formulas remain unchanged. It must
be installed before any count cell is evaluated so no cached old interval
object crosses runtimes. Independent review and bounded whole-cell tests
are pending. It is currently an experiment only.
